 ## Background
-Regularization and loss functions in NeRF optimization originated from the need to resolve the shape-radiance ambiguity and artifacts like "floaters" in sparse-view settings. Early works focused on photometric losses, but as NeRFs were applied to fewer views and generative tasks, methods like RegNeRF introduced geometric smoothness and Normalizing Flow priors [S1, 8582-regnerf.md]. Subsequent developments incorporated diffusion models for generative priors [S1, 7381-diffusion-regularized-nerf.md], semantic consistency via CLIP [S1, S3, 11955-semantic-consistency-loss-clip-based.md], and stochastic structural similarity for better high-frequency detail preservation [S1, 10895-s3im-stochastic-structural-similarity.md]. These methods are widely used in novel view synthesis, 3D generation, and inverse rendering.
+The concept of regularization in NeRF training emerged alongside the original NeRF paper [S1, 8582-regnerf.md] to address the ill-posed nature of the inverse rendering problem. Early methods relied on simple photometric losses (MSE/L1). As the field moved towards sparse-view and few-shot scenarios, methods like RegNeRF introduced geometric smoothness and normalizing flow priors to constrain unobserved regions [S1]. The integration of diffusion models for regularization (Diffusion-regularized NeRF) represents a more recent development, leveraging large-scale generative priors to enforce geometric plausibility [S1, S2]. Semantic consistency losses using CLIP have been adopted to handle photometric ambiguity in unstructured scenes [S1, S3]. Volumetric supervision has been adapted from tomography to dynamic NeRFs [S1].
 
